<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albertine Rift</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Albertine Graben</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Albertine Rift is the western branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-african-rift">East African Rift</a>, running from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-albert">Lake Albert</a> south through Lakes Edward, Kivu and Tanganyika along the borders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a> with Uganda, Rwanda, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burundi">Burundi</a> and Tanzania. Its Ugandan section, the Albertine Graben around Lake Albert, holds commercial oil reserves discovered in 2006, which led to plans for an export pipeline through Tanzania. The rift also holds some of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>'s richest wildlife.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albertine-rift.jpg" alt="Albertine Rift" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christoph Hormann · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Albertine_Rift,_East_African_Rift_(artificial_rendering).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The rift runs from the northern end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-albert">Lake Albert</a> to the southern end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tanganyika">Lake Tanganyika</a>, and the name covers both the valley and the mountains around it. It is the product of tectonic movements that are gradually splitting the Somali Plate away from the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. The surrounding mountains are uplifted Pre-Cambrian basement rocks, overlaid in places by recent volcanic rocks.</p><p>Two ranges cross the northern part: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rwenzori-mountains">Rwenzori Mountains</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-albert">Lake Albert</a> and Lake Rutanzige, formerly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-edward">Lake Edward</a>, and the Virunga Mountains between Lake Rutanzige and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-kivu">Lake Kivu</a>. The Rwenzori, about 120 km long and 65 km wide, have been identified with Ptolemy's Mountains of the Moon and rise to 5,119 m at Mount Stanley. The Virunga Massif on the border of Rwanda and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a> has eight volcanoes, of which Nyamuragira and Nyiragongo are still highly active. Further south stand the Itombwe Mountains, Mount Bururi, the Kungwe-Mahale Mountains, Mount Kabobo and the Marungu Mountains.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Virungas divide the Nile Basin to the north and east from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-basin">Congo Basin</a> to the west and south. Lake Rutanzige drains north through the Semliki River into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-albert">Lake Albert</a>, which receives the Victoria Nile and sends out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Nile</a>. Lake Kivu drains south through the Ruzizi River into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tanganyika">Lake Tanganyika</a>, which flows to the Congo through the Lukuga River. This system seems to be recent: eruptions of the Virunga volcanoes probably blocked Lake Kivu's northward outflow into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-edward">Lake Edward</a> and turned it south. Before then Lake Tanganyika may have lost water only by evaporation, and the later Lukuga let species of the Congo Basin into a lake that had had its own fauna.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Albertine Rift montane forests are important eco-regions, with transitional forest from about 1,000 m, montane forest from about 1,600 m to 3,500 m, bamboo and elfin forest above 2,400 m, and heather and grasses higher still. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deforestation">Deforestation</a> for farmland, illegal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> extraction and artisanal gold mining threaten them.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Albertine Rift” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
